 4. cite32†6.4 The global bounds L43:     5. cite33†6.5 Computational verification L44:   8. cite34†7 Lower bounds for six and seven petals L45:     1. cite35†7.1 Six petals L46:     2. cite36†7.2 Seven petals L47:     3. cite37†7.3 Verification of the finite witness L48:   9. cite38†8 Formal verification in Lean L49:   10. cite39†A Alternative capacity recurrence L50:   11. cite40†References L51: cite41†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L52: \nL53: arXiv:2609.06175v1 [math.CO] 05 Sep 2026\nL54: # Sunflower-Free Uniform Families: Recursive Constructions and Explicit Bounds\nL55: \nL56: Edward Axante ^{†}^{†}thanks: Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science, University of Bucharest, Romania.    Cristian Budala^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1    David Chitic^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1    Bogdan Dumitru^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1    and Mihai Nacu^{1}^{1}footnotemark: 1\nL57: ###### Abstract\nL58: Let $f(w,k)$ be the maximum size of a $w$-uniform family containing no sunflower with $k$ petals. We introduce a recursive construction for sunflower-free families and use it to obtain a general lower bound on the exponential growth rate of $f(w,k)$. We also prove a general upper bound for $3$-uniform families with at least four petals. Our results give $39\\leq f(3,4)\\leq 49$, $f(3,5)\\leq 146$, $153\\leq f(3,6)\\leq 255$, $259\\leq f(3,7)\\leq 474$, and $54\\leq f(4,3)\\leq 83$.\nL59: In addition, we prove that the maximum size of an intersecting $4$-uniform family containing no sunflower with three petals is $27$. The upper bounds $49$ and $83$ are computer-assisted. The finite lower bounds come from explicit constructions.\nL60: ## 1 Introduction\nL61: A sunflower with $k$ petals, or $k$-sunflower, is a collection of $k$ distinct sets whose pairwise intersections are equal. Their common intersection is called the core and may be empty. Let $f(w,k)$ denote the maximum size of a family of distinct $w$-element sets containing no $k$-sunflower. The sunflower conjecture of Erdős and Rado asks whether, for every fixed $k$, there is a constant $C_{k}$ such that $f(w,k)\\leq C_{k}^{w}$ for every $w$ [cite42†11 ].\nL62: Alweiss, Lovett, Wu, and Zhang substantially improved the classical upper bound [cite43†6 ], and Bell, Chueluecha, and Warnke subsequently proved $f(w,k)\\leq(Ck\\log w)^{w}$ for an absolute constant $C$ and all $w,k\\geq 2$ [cite44†7 ].\nL63: Our lower-bound arguments keep track not only of the absence of sunflowers but also of the maximum number of pairwise disjoint members. Controlling this matching number rules out sunflowers with empty core. Within this framework, we construct larger sunflower-free families from smaller ones by attaching subsets of an auxiliary set. The resulting recurrence leads to an explicit general lower bound on the exponential growth rate of $f(w,k)$.\nL64: An alternative construction based on a bounded-degree auxiliary graph is included in the appendix.\nL65: Abbott and Hanson introduced the study of sunflower-free families with a bounded matching number and showed how families on disjoint ground sets can be combined to obtain lower bounds for $f(w,k)$ [cite45†4 ]. For three petals, our main recurrence follows from a multiplication theorem of Abbott and Exoo [cite46†5 ]. We extend this recurrence to arbitrary numbers of petals and all admissible matching-number bounds.\nL66: To our knowledge, this recurrence, the alternative recurrence in the appendix, and the resulting exponential lower bound are new. Abbott and Hanson raised the problem of proving recurrences for $\\psi$ and reported no success [cite45†4 , p. 11]. The closest prior forms are an additive recurrence of Abbott and Exoo for three petals [cite46†5 , Theorem 3] and an edge-attachment product of Frankl and Wang [cite47†13 , Proposition 2.1].\nL67: For $k-1\\geq 3w(m-1)$, Frankl and Wang determine $\\psi(w,k,m)$ exactly [cite47†13 , Theorem 1.11]; the bounds proved here concern fixed $k$ and growing $w$, outside that regime.\nL68: For upper bounds on families of triples, we begin with a largest collection of pairwise disjoint triples and classify all other members by how they intersect its union. This reduces the problem to degree and matching bounds for ordinary graphs, to which we apply the theorem of Chvátal and Hanson [cite48†9 ]. The resulting argument gives a general upper bound for $f(3,k)$ when $k\\geq 4$. Separate finite analyses sharpen the result for $f(3,4)$ and give the upper bound for $f(4,3)$.\nL69: cite49†Table 1 compares the numerical bounds obtained in this paper with previously published bounds. The two published endpoints shown for $f(3,7)$ follow from Proposition 2.1 and Theorem 1.14, respectively, of Frankl and Wang [cite47†13 ].\nL70: Table 1: Published comparisons and bounds proved in this paper.\nL71: Quantity  | Published comparison  | Bound proved here\nL72: --- | --- | ---\nL73: $f(3,4)$  | $38$ [cite46†5 ] $\\leq f(3,4)$  | $\\leq$  | $69$ [cite50†3 ]  | $39\\leq f(3,4)$  | $\\leq$  | $49$\nL74: $f(3,5)$  | $f(3,5)$  | $\\leq$  | $180$ [cite50†3 ]  | $f(3,5)$  | $\\leq$  | $146$\nL75: $f(3,6)$  | $146$ [cite46†5 ] $\\leq f(3,6)$  | $\\leq$  | $305$ [cite50†3 ]  | $153\\leq f(3,6)$  | $\\leq$  | $255$\nL76: $f(3,7)$  | $252\\leq f(3,7)$  | $\\leq$  | $498$ [cite47†13 ]  | $259\\leq f(3,7)$  | $\\leq$  | $474$\nL77: $f(4,3)$  | $54$ [cite45†4 ] $\\leq f(4,3)$  | $\\leq$  | $142$ [cite50†3 ]  | $54\\leq f(4,3)$  | $\\leq$  | $83$\nL78: The upper bounds $f(3,4)\\leq 49$ and $f(4,3)\\leq 83$ are computer-assisted and follow from exhaustive finite enumerations. The lower bounds $f(3,4)\\geq 39$ and $f(3,7)\\geq 259$ are established by explicit finite families. We also determine exactly the maximum size of an intersecting $4$-uniform family containing no $3$-sunflower: it is $27$.\nL79: The main combinatorial arguments of cite7†sections 2 , cite12†3 and cite16†4 , the appendix capacity recurrence, and every explicit finite lower-bound witness have additionally been formalised and machine-checked in the Lean 4 proof assistant (cite38†section 8 ).^{1}^{1} 1 Code, data, and the Lean development are available at cite51†https://github.com/bogdan27182/sunflower-paper†github.com .\nL80: ## 2 Notation and preliminary facts\nL81: \nL82: This section fixes the notation and records the basic facts used in the paper. We introduce links and the matching-constrained extremal quantity, determine the cases of uniformity one and two, and recall the product inequality used to define the exponential growth rate.\nL83: ### 2.1 Set families\nL84: \nL85: All set families in this paper are finite and have distinct members. All graphs are finite and simple. For a finite set $X$, write\nL86: \nL87:  | $$\\binom{X}{w}=\\{A\\subseteq X:|A|=w\\}.$$  |\nL88: \nL89: A family $\\mathcal{F}$ is $w$-uniform if $\\mathcal{F}\\subseteq\\binom{X}{w}$ for some finite set $X$. Its support is\nL90: \nL91:  | $$\\operatorname{supp}(\\mathcal{F})=\\bigcup_{F\\in\\mathcal{F}}F.$$  |\nL92: \nL93: If distinct sets $A_{1},\\ldots,A_{k}$ satisfy\nL94: \nL95:  | $$A_{i}\\cap A_{j}=C\\qquad(1\\leq i<j\\leq k),$$  |\nL96: then they form a $k$-sunflower with core $C$. The core may be empty. The sets $A_{i}\\setminus C$ are its petals. For $w\\geq 1$ and $k\\geq 2$, define\nL97: \nL98:  | $$f(w,k)=\\max\\bigl\\{|\\mathcal{F}|:\\mathcal{F}\\text{ is $w$-uniform and contains no\nL99: $k$-sunflower}\\bigr\\}.$$  |  | (2.1)\nL100: \nL101: The Erdős–Rado sunflower lemma makes this maximum finite [cite42†11 ].\nL102: ###### Lemma 2.1 (Occurrence rule).\nL103: \nL104: Let $A_{1},\\ldots,A_{k}$ form a sunflower. Every point in $A_{1}\\cup\\cdots\\cup A_{k}$ belongs to exactly one member or to all $k$ members.\nL105: \nL106: ###### Proof.\nL107: \nL108: Suppose that a point belongs to two members. It then belongs to their intersection, which is the core. Hence it belongs to every member. ∎\nL109: \nL110: The core of a sunflower of distinct $w$-element sets has size at most $w-1$. In particular, the possible core sizes for a sunflower of triples are zero, one, and two.\nL111: ### 2.2 Matchings, degrees, and links\nL112: \nL113: A matching in a family is a subfamily of pairwise disjoint members. The matching number $\\nu(\\mathcal{F})$ is the largest size of a matching in $\\mathcal{F}$. A family is intersecting if every two distinct members meet. Equivalently, $\\nu(\\mathcal{F})\\leq 1$. Since a matching of size $k$ is a $k$-sunflower with empty core, every $k$-sunflower-free family satisfies\nL114: \nL115:  | $$\\nu(\\mathcal{F})\\leq k-1.$$  |  | (2.2)\nL116: \nL117: For $m\\geq 2$, define\nL118:  | $$\\psi(w,k,m)=\\max\\bigl\\{|\\mathcal{F}|:\\mathcal{F}\\text{ is $w$-uniform, contains no\nL119: $k$-sunflower, and }\\nu(\\mathcal{F})\\leq m-1\\bigr\\}.$$  |  | (2.3)\nL120: \nL121: This matching-constrained quantity was introduced by Abbott and Hanson [cite45†4 ]. In particular, $\\psi(w,k,2)$ is the maximum over intersecting $w$-uniform families without a $k$-sunflower, while $\\psi(w,k,k)=f(w,k)$.\nL122: \nL123: For a set $S$, define its degree in $\\mathcal{F}$ by\nL124: \nL125:  | $$d_{\\mathcal{F}}(S)=|\\{F\\in\\mathcal{F}:S\\subseteq F\\}|.$$  |\nL126: For $S=\\{x\\}$, write $d_{\\mathcal{F}}(x)$. The link of $S$ is\nL127: \nL128:  | $$\\operatorname{Lk}_{\\mathcal{F}}(S)=\\{F\\setminus S:F\\in\\mathcal{F},\\ S\\subseteq F\\}.$$  |\nL129: ###### Lemma 2.2 (Link bound).\nL130: \nL131: Let $\\mathcal{F}$ be a $w$-uniform family without a $k$-sunflower. If $|S|=s<w$, then $\\operatorname{Lk}_{\\mathcal{F}}(S)$ is a $(w-s)$-uniform family without a $k$-sunflower. Consequently,\nL132: \nL133:  | $$d_{\\mathcal{F}}(S)=|\\operatorname{Lk}_{\\mathcal{F}}(S)|\\leq f(w-s,k).$$  |  | (2.4)\nL134: ###### Proof.\nL135: \nL136: The members of $\\operatorname{Lk}_{\\mathcal{F}}(S)$ are distinct. If $k$ of them formed a sunflower with core $C$, adjoining $S$ would give a sunflower in $\\mathcal{F}$ with core $C\\cup S$. ∎\nL137: \nL138: Two families $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ are cross-intersecting if $A\\cap B\\neq\\varnothing$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal{A}$ and $B\\in\\mathcal{B}$. A collection of families is pairwise cross-intersecting if each pair in the collection is cross-intersecting.\nL139: ### 2.3 The graph case\nL140: \nL141: For integers $D,q\\geq 1$, let $\\operatorname{CH}(D,q)$ be the maximum number of edges in a graph with maximum degree at most $D$ and matching number at most $q$. The Chvátal–Hanson theorem [cite48†9 ] gives\nL142: \nL143:  | $$\\operatorname{CH}(D,q)=Dq+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{D}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\left\\lfloor\\frac{q}{\\lceil D/2\\rceil}\\right\\rfloor.$$  |  | (2.5)\nL144: \nL145: ###### Lemma 2.3 (Uniformities one and two).\nL146: \nL147: For every $k\\geq 2$,\nL148: \nL149:  | $$f(1,k)=k-1,\\qquad f(2,k)=\\operatorname{CH}(k-1,k-1).$$  |  | (2.6)\nL150: ###### Proof.\nL151: \nL152: A family of distinct singletons contains a $k$-sunflower exactly when it has at least $k$ members. This gives the first equality.\nL153: \nL154: View a $2$-uniform family as a graph. A sunflower of distinct edges has empty core or a one-point core. These two cases are a matching and a set of edges incident with one vertex. The graph is therefore $k$-sunflower-free exactly when its matching number and maximum degree are both at most $k-1$. Equation (cite52†2.5 ) gives the second equality. ∎\nL155: ### 2.4 Products\nL156: \nL157: Abbott proved the following product inequality [cite53†1 ]. Frankl and Wang later proved a vector-valued generalization [cite47†13 ].\nL158: \nL159: Let $X$ and $Y$ be disjoint. For $\\mathcal{A}\\subseteq\\binom{X}{u}$ and $\\mathcal{B}\\subseteq\\binom{Y}{v}$, define\nL160: \nL161:  | $$\\mathcal{A}*\\mathcal{B}=\\{A\\cup B:A\\in\\mathcal{A},\\ B\\in\\mathcal{B}\\}.$$  |\nL162: \nL163: This family is $(u+v)$-uniform and has size $|\\mathcal{A}||\\mathcal{B}|$.\nL164: ###### Lemma 2.4 (Product construction).\nL165: \nL166: If $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ contain no $k$-sunflower, then $\\mathcal{A}*\\mathcal{B}$ contains no $k$-sunflower. Hence\nL167: \nL168:  | $$f(u+v,k)\\geq f(u,k)f(v,k).$$  |  | (2.7)\nL169: ###### Proof.\nL170: \nL171: Suppose that $A_{i}\\cup B_{i}$, for $1\\leq i\\leq k$, form a sunflower in the product family. Intersecting with $X$ and $Y$ shows that the pairwise intersections among the $A_{i}$ are equal, and the same holds among the $B_{i}$.\nL172: If two $A_{i}$ are equal, their common pairwise intersection is that whole set. It is contained in every other $A_{j}$. Uniformity then implies that all $A_{i}$ are equal. Otherwise the $A_{i}$ are distinct and form a $k$-sunflower in $\\mathcal{A}$. The latter case is impossible, so all $A_{i}$ are equal. The same argument shows that all $B_{i}$ are equal. The $k$ product members are then equal, a contradiction.\nL173: Taking $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ to be extremal families on disjoint supports, the product family has $f(u,k)f(v,k)$ members, which proves cite54†equation 2.7 . ∎\nL174: \nL175: Since $\\log f(w,k)$ is superadditive, Fekete’s lemma [cite55†12 ] and cite54†equation 2.7 give the extended limit\nL176: \nL177:  | $$B(k)=\\lim_{w\\to\\infty}f(w,k)^{1/w}=\\sup_{w\\geq 1}f(w,k)^{1/w}.$$  |  | (2.8)\nL178: \nL179: The value in cite56†equation 2.8 may be infinite. The sunflower conjecture states that it is finite for every fixed $k$.\nL180: ## 3 A matching-constrained construction\nL181: \nL182: We first construct larger sunflower-free families from smaller ones while controlling their matching numbers. We then specialize the resulting recurrence and combine it with a simple splitting argument to obtain a recurrence involving only $f(w,k)$. Finally, we solve this recurrence to derive an explicit lower bound on the exponential growth rate of $f(w,k)$. An alternative recurrence using a bounded-degree auxiliary graph is recorded in the appendix.\nL183: ### 3.1 Block-size recurrence\nL184: \nL185: The construction builds a $w$-uniform sunflower-free family from smaller families by adjoining either one auxiliary point or a larger auxiliary block, while preserving the required matching-number bound.\nL186: \nL187: ###### Theorem 3.1 (Block-size recurrence).\nL188: \nL189: Let $k\\geq 3$ and $2\\leq m\\leq k-1$. Put\nL190: \nL191:  | $$t=k-m+1.$$  |\nL192: \nL193: For every $w\\geq t+1$,\nL194: \nL195:  | $$\\psi(w,k,m)\\geq(k-1)\\psi(w-1,k,m)+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{t}\\right\\rfloor f(w-t,k).$$  |  | (3.1)\nL196: ###### Proof.\nL197: \nL198: Take pairwise disjoint sets $X,Y,Z$, where $|Y|=k-1$. On $X$, take a $(w-1)$-uniform family $\\mathcal{A}$ with no $k$-sunflower such that\nL199: \nL200:  | $$|\\mathcal{A}|=\\psi(w-1,k,m),\\qquad\\nu(\\mathcal{A})\\leq m-1.$$  |\nL201: Let $\\mathcal{P}$ consist of $\\lfloor(k-1)/t\\rfloor$ pairwise disjoint $t$-subsets of $Y$. For each $p\\in\\mathcal{P}$, take a $(w-t)$-uniform family $\\mathcal{E}_{p}$ on $Z$ with no $k$-sunflower and with $|\\mathcal{E}_{p}|=f(w-t,k)$. The families $\\mathcal{E}_{p}$ may have the same support. Define\nL202: \nL203:  | $$\\begin{split}\\mathcal{F}={}&\\{T\\cup\\{y\\}:T\\in\\mathcal{A},\\ y\\in Y\\}\\\\\nL204: &{}\\cup\\{p\\cup S:p\\in\\mathcal{P},\\ S\\in\\mathcal{E}_{p}\\}.\\end{split}$$  |  | (3.2)\nL205: The members in the first and second lines meet $Y$ in one and $t$ points, respectively. Since $t\\geq 2$, the two lines are disjoint. All members within each line are distinct. Consequently,\nL206: \nL207:  | $$|\\mathcal{F}|=|Y||\\mathcal{A}|+\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal{P}}|\\mathcal{E}_{p}|=(k-1)\\psi(w-1,k,m)+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{t}\\right\\rfloor f(w-t,k),$$  |\nL208: \nL209: which is the right-hand side of cite57†equation 3.1 .\nL210: Consider a matching in $\\mathcal{F}$ with $q$ members from the first line and $b$ members from the second. If $b=0$, the $X$-parts form a matching in $\\mathcal{A}$, so $q\\leq m-1$. If $b\\geq 1$, then, because the matching members are pairwise disjoint, their intersections with $Y$ are also pairwise disjoint, and\nL211: \nL212:  | $$q+tb\\leq k-1.$$  |\nL213: \nL214: It follows that\nL215: \nL216:  | $$q+b\\leq k-1-(t-1)b\\leq k-t=m-1.$$  |\nL217: \nL218: Hence $\\nu(\\mathcal{F})\\leq m-1$.\nL219: Suppose that $F_{1},\\ldots,F_{k}$ form a sunflower in $\\mathcal{F}$. Every $F_{i}$ meets the $(k-1)$-point set $Y$. By the pigeonhole principle, some $y\\in Y$ therefore belongs to at least two of them. By cite58†lemma 2.1 , it belongs to all of them.\nL220: \nL221: If all $F_{i}$ come from the first line of cite59†equation 3.2 , write $F_{i}=T_{i}\\cup\\{y\\}$. Deleting $y$ gives a $k$-sunflower of distinct members of $\\mathcal{A}$.\nL222: If all $F_{i}$ come from the second line, every corresponding block in $\\mathcal{P}$ contains $y$. The blocks in $\\mathcal{P}$ are disjoint, so all $F_{i}$ use the same block $p$. Deleting $p$ gives a $k$-sunflower of distinct members of $\\mathcal{E}_{p}$.\nL223: It remains to consider a sunflower using both lines. A member from the first line and a member from the second intersect exactly in $\\{y\\}$. The core is therefore $\\{y\\}$. The $X$-parts of the members from the first line form a matching in $\\mathcal{A}$, so there are at most $m-1$ such members. All members from the second line contain $y$. Since the blocks in $\\mathcal{P}$ are disjoint, they all use the same block $p$.\nL224: The set $p\\setminus\\{y\\}$ is nonempty because $t\\geq 2$, so there is at most one member from the second line. The sunflower has at most $m\\leq k-1$ members, a contradiction. ∎\nL225: The case $m=k-1$ gives the recurrence used for the exponential estimate.\nL226: ###### Corollary 3.2.\nL227: \nL228: For every $k\\geq 3$ and $w\\geq 3$,\nL229: \nL230:  | $$\\psi(w,k,k-1)\\geq(k-1)\\psi(w-1,k,k-1)+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor f(w-2,k).$$  |  | (3.3)\nL231: \nL232: ### 3.2 Unrestricted families\nL233: \nL234: The following inequalities connect the matching-constrained quantities to $f(w,k)$. The second is a case of the Abbott–Hanson disjoint-support split [cite45†4 ].\nL235: ###### Lemma 3.3 (Star and split).\nL236: \nL237: For every $k\\geq 3$ and $w\\geq 2$,\nL238: \nL239:  | $\\displaystyle\\psi(w,k,2)$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq f(w-1,k),$  |  | (3.4)\nL240:  | $\\displaystyle f(w,k)$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq\\psi(w,k,k-1)+\\psi(w,k,2).$  |  | (3.5)\nL241: ###### Proof.\nL242: \nL243: For cite60†equation 3.4 , take an extremal $(w-1)$-uniform family with $f(w-1,k)$ members and add one new point to every member. The resulting family has the same size and is intersecting. Deleting the new point from a sunflower would give a sunflower in the original family, so the resulting family is counted by $\\psi(w,k,2)$. This proves cite60†equation 3.4 .\nL244: For cite61†equation 3.5 , take extremal families attaining $\\psi(w,k,k-1)$ and $\\psi(w,k,2)$ and place them on disjoint supports. Their union has size $\\psi(w,k,k-1)+\\psi(w,k,2)$. A sunflower with nonempty core cannot use members from both supports. A sunflower with empty core would be a matching of size $k$, but the matching number of the union is at most\nL245: \nL246:  | $$(k-2)+1=k-1.$$  |\nL247: \nL248: Thus the union contains no $k$-sunflower, which proves cite61†equation 3.5 . ∎\nL249: \nL250: For $k\\geq 4$, Frankl and Wang proved\nL251:  | $$\\psi(3,k,2)=f(2,k)$$  |  | (3.6)\nL252: \nL253: [cite47†13 , Theorem 1.15]. Thus the star bound cite60†equation 3.4 is tight when $w=3$.\nL254: ### 3.3 Exponential rate\nL255: \nL256: Combining cite62†corollaries 3.2 and cite63†3.3 yields simultaneous recursive lower bounds for $\\psi(w,k,k-1)$ and $f(w,k)$. We use $a_{w}$ to track lower bounds for $\\psi(w,k,k-1)$ and $b_{w}$ to track lower bounds for $f(w,k)$.\nL257: ###### Proposition 3.4 (Recursive lower bounds).\nL258: \nL259: Fix $k\\geq 3$. Let $a_{3},b_{2},b_{3}$ be positive integers satisfying\nL260: \nL261:  | $$a_{3}\\leq\\psi(3,k,k-1),\\qquad b_{2}\\leq f(2,k),\\qquad b_{3}\\leq f(3,k).$$  |\nL262: \nL263: For $w\\geq 4$, define\nL264: \nL265:  | $\\displaystyle a_{w}$  | $\\displaystyle=(k-1)a_{w-1}+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor b_{w-2},$  |  | (3.7)\nL266:  | $\\displaystyle b_{w}$  | $\\displaystyle=a_{w}+b_{w-1}.$  |  | (3.8)\nL267: \nL268: Then\nL269: \nL270:  | $$a_{w}\\leq\\psi(w,k,k-1),\\qquad b_{w}\\leq f(w,k)\\qquad(w\\geq 4).$$  |\nL271: ###### Proof.\nL272: \nL273: We prove both inequalities simultaneously by induction. For $w\\geq 4$, assume that\nL274: \nL275:  | $$a_{w-1}\\leq\\psi(w-1,k,k-1),\\qquad b_{w-2}\\leq f(w-2,k),\\qquad b_{w-1}\\leq f(w-1,k).$$  |\nL276: \nL277: When $w=4$, these are precisely the three assumed initial bounds. By cite64†equation 3.3 ,\nL278: \nL279:  | $\\displaystyle\\psi(w,k,k-1)$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq(k-1)\\psi(w-1,k,k-1)+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor f(w-2,k)$  |\nL280:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\geq(k-1)a_{w-1}+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor b_{w-2}=a_{w}.$  |\nL281: Using cite60†equations 3.4 and cite61†3.5 , we then obtain\nL282: \nL283:  | $\\displaystyle f(w,k)$  | $\\displaystyle\\geq\\psi(w,k,k-1)+\\psi(w,k,2)$  |\nL284:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\geq a_{w}+f(w-1,k)$  |\nL285:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\geq a_{w}+b_{w-1}=b_{w}.$  |\nL286: \nL287: These two bounds provide the induction hypotheses at the next index. ∎\nL288: \nL289: The two recurrences reduce to one recurrence for $b_{w}$. For $w\\geq 5$, cite65†equation 3.8 at index $w-1$ gives\nL290: \nL291:  | $$a_{w-1}=b_{w-1}-b_{w-2}.$$  |\nL292: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA1K Agentic SuperMath · Transversal Six, Finite Obstructions and Boundary Profiles in Rank-Four Sunflower-Free Families (https://a1k.ai/agentic-math/rank-four-sunflower-free-families)\nciteturn12view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://a1k.ai/agentic-math/rank-four-sunflower-free-families\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 317\n"
